dsm5:Fetishistic_Disorder a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Fetishistic Disorder"@en ;
    med:hasCriterion dsm5:Fetishistic_Disorder_Criterion_A,
        dsm5:Fetishistic_Disorder_Criterion_B,
        dsm5:Fetishistic_Disorder_Criterion_C ;
    med:hasSpecifierText """Although individuals with fetishistic disorder may report intense and
recurrent sexual arousal to inanimate objects or a specific body part,
it is not unusual for non--mutually exclusive combinations of
fetishistic sexual interests to occur. Thus, an individual may have
fetishistic disorder associated with an inanimate object (e.g., female
undergarments) or an exclusive focus on an intensely eroticized body
part (e.g., feet, hair), or his or her fetishistic interest may meet
criteria for various combinations of these specifiers (e.g., socks,
shoes, and feet)."""@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf dsm5:MentalDisorder ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fetishistic Disorder"@en .

dsm5:Frotteuristic_Disorder a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Frotteuristic Disorder"@en ;
    med:hasCriterion dsm5:Frotteuristic_Disorder_Criterion_A,
        dsm5:Frotteuristic_Disorder_Criterion_B ;
    med:hasSpecifierText """The "in remission" specifier does not address the continued presence or
absence of frotteurism per se, which may still be present after
behaviors and distress have remitted."""@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf dsm5:MentalDisorder ;
    skos:prefLabel "Frotteuristic Disorder"@en .

dsm5:Functional_Neurological_Symptom_Disorder_Conversion_Disorder a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Functional Neurological Symptom Disorder (Conversion Disorder)"@en ;
    med:hasCriterion dsm5:Functional_Neurological_Symptom_Disorder_Conversion_Disorder_Criterion_A,
        dsm5:Functional_Neurological_Symptom_Disorder_Conversion_Disorder_Criterion_B,
        dsm5:Functional_Neurological_Symptom_Disorder_Conversion_Disorder_Criterion_C,
        dsm5:Functional_Neurological_Symptom_Disorder_Conversion_Disorder_Criterion_D ;
    rdfs:subClassOf dsm5:MentalDisorder ;
    skos:prefLabel "Functional Neurological Symptom Disorder (Conversion Disorder)"@en .

dsm5:Gambling_Disorder a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Gambling Disorder"@en ;
    med:hasCriterion dsm5:Gambling_Disorder_Criterion_A,
        dsm5:Gambling_Disorder_Criterion_B ;
    med:hasSeverityLevel dsm5:Gambling_Disorder__Severity_0,
        dsm5:Gambling_Disorder__Severity_1,
        dsm5:Gambling_Disorder__Severity_2 ;
    med:hasSpecifierText """Severity is based on the number of criteria endorsed. Individuals with
mild gambling disorder may exhibit only 4--5 of the criteria, with the
most frequently endorsed criteria usually related to preoccupation with
gambling and "chasing" losses. Individuals with moderately severe
gambling disorder exhibit more of the criteria (i.e., 6--7). Individuals
with the most severe form will exhibit all or most of the nine criteria
(i.e., 8--9). Jeopardizing relationships or career opportunities because
of gambling and relying on others to provide money for gambling losses
are typically the least often endorsed criteria and most often occur
among those with more severe gambling disorder. Furthermore, individuals
presenting for treatment of gambling disorder typically have moderate to
severe forms of the disorder."""@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf dsm5:MentalDisorder ;
    skos:prefLabel "Gambling Disorder"@en .

dsm5:Gender_Dysphoria a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Gender Dysphoria"@en ;
    med:hasCriterion dsm5:Gender_Dysphoria_Criterion_A,
        dsm5:Gender_Dysphoria_Criterion_B ;
    med:hasSpecifierText """The specifier "with a disorder/difference of sex development" should be
used in the context of individuals who have a specific and codable
disorder/difference of sex development documented in their medical
record.

The "posttransition" specifier may be used in the context of continuing
treatment procedures that serve to support the new gender assignment."""@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf dsm5:MentalDisorder ;
    skos:prefLabel "Gender Dysphoria"@en .

