dsm5:Avoidant_Restrictive_Food_Intake_Disorder_Criterion_B a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder — Criterion B"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The disturbance is not better explained by lack of available food or by an associated culturally sanctioned practice"@en .

dsm5:Avoidant_Restrictive_Food_Intake_Disorder_Criterion_C a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder — Criterion C"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The eating disturbance does not occur exclusively during the course of anorexia nervosa or bulimia nervosa, and there is no evidence of a disturbance in the way in which one's body weight or shape is experienced"@en .

dsm5:Avoidant_Restrictive_Food_Intake_Disorder_Criterion_D a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder — Criterion D"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The eating disturbance is not attributable to a concurrent medical condition or not better explained by another mental disorder. When the eating disturbance occurs in the context of another condition or disorder, the severity of the eating disturbance exceeds that routinely associated with the condition or disorder and warrants additional clinical attention"@en .

dsm5:Binge_Eating_Disorder_Criterion_A a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Binge-Eating Disorder — Criterion A"@en ;
    med:hasRequiredDuration "PT2H"^^xsd:duration ;
    rdfs:comment "Recurrent episodes of binge eating. An episode of binge eating is characterized by both of the following Eating, in a discrete period of time (e.g., within any 2-hour period), an amount of food that is definitely larger than what most people would eat in a similar period of time under similar circumstances. A sense of lack of control over eating during the episode (e.g., a feeling that one cannot stop eating or control what or how much one is eating)."@en .

dsm5:Binge_Eating_Disorder_Criterion_B a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Binge-Eating Disorder — Criterion B"@en ;
    med:hasMinimumSymptomCount 3 ;
    med:hasSymptom dsm5:Binge_Eating_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_0000716,
        dsm5:Binge_Eating_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_0100738 ;
    rdfs:comment "The binge-eating episodes are associated with three (or more) of the following Eating much more rapidly than normal. Eating until feeling uncomfortably full. Eating large amounts of food when not feeling physically hungry. Eating alone because of feeling embarrassed by how much one is eating. Feeling disgusted with oneself, depressed, or very guilty afterward."@en .

dsm5:Binge_Eating_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_0000716 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Depression"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0000716 ;
    rdfs:comment "The phrase describes feelings of being depressed, which aligns with the HPO term for Depression."@en .

dsm5:Binge_Eating_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_0100738 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Abnormal eating behavior"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0100738 ;
    rdfs:comment "The phrase describes a specific abnormal eating behavior, which aligns with the term 'Abnormal eating behavior'."@en .

dsm5:Binge_Eating_Disorder_Criterion_C a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Binge-Eating Disorder — Criterion C"@en ;
    med:hasSymptom dsm5:Binge_Eating_Disorder_Criterion_C__HP_0100739 ;
    rdfs:comment "Marked distress regarding binge eating is present"@en .

dsm5:Binge_Eating_Disorder_Criterion_C__HP_0100739 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Bulimia"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0100739 ;
    rdfs:comment "The phrase refers to distress related to binge eating, which aligns with the term for Bulimia, a disorder characterized by binge eating and associated distress."@en .

dsm5:Binge_Eating_Disorder_Criterion_D a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Binge-Eating Disorder — Criterion D"@en ;
    med:hasRequiredDuration "P3M"^^xsd:duration ;
    med:hasSymptom dsm5:Binge_Eating_Disorder_Criterion_D__HP_0100739 ;
    rdfs:comment "The binge eating occurs, on average, at least once a week for 3 months"@en .

dsm5:Binge_Eating_Disorder_Criterion_D__HP_0100739 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Bulimia"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0100739 ;
    rdfs:comment "The phrase describes binge eating behavior, which aligns with the HPO term for Bulimia."@en .

dsm5:Binge_Eating_Disorder_Criterion_E a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Binge-Eating Disorder — Criterion E"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The binge eating is not associated with the recurrent use of inappropriate compensatory behavior as in bulimia nervosa and does not occur exclusively during the course of bulimia nervosa or anorexia nervosa"@en .

dsm5:Binge_Eating_Disorder__Severity_0 a dsm5:SeverityLevel,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Mild:** 1--3 binge-eating episodes per week."@en .

dsm5:Binge_Eating_Disorder__Severity_1 a dsm5:SeverityLevel,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Moderate:** 4--7 binge-eating episodes per week."@en .

dsm5:Binge_Eating_Disorder__Severity_2 a dsm5:SeverityLevel,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Severe:** 8--13 binge-eating episodes per week."@en .

dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_A a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Bipolar II Disorder — Criterion A"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A distinct period of abnormally and persistently elevated, expansive, or irritable mood and abnormally and persistently increased activity or energy, lasting at least 4 consecutive days and present most of the day, nearly every day"@en .

dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_B a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Bipolar II Disorder — Criterion B"@en ;
    med:hasMinimumSymptomCount 3 ;
    med:hasRequiredDuration "PT3H"^^xsd:duration ;
    med:hasSymptom dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_0000713,
        dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_0000736,
        dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_0006979,
        dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_0033844,
        dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_4000092,
        dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_5200270 ;
    rdfs:comment "During the period of mood disturbance and increased energy and activity, three (or more) of the following symptoms have persisted (four if the mood is only irritable), represent a noticeable change from usual behavior, and have been present to a significant degree Inflated self-esteem or grandiosity. Decreased need for sleep (e.g., feels rested after only 3 hours of sleep). More talkative than usual or pressure to keep talking. Flight of ideas or subjective experience that thoughts are racing. Distractibility (i.e., attention too easily drawn to unimportant or irrelevant external stimuli), as reported or observed. Increase in goal-directed activity (either socially, at work or school, or sexually) or psychomotor agitation. Excessive involvement in activities that have a high potential for painful consequences (e.g., engaging in unrestrained buying sprees, sexual indiscretions, or foolish business investments)."@en .

dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_0000713 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Agitation"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0000713 ;
    rdfs:comment "Agitation is a direct match for psychomotor agitation mentioned in the DSM criterion."@en .

dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_0000736 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Short attention span"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0000736 ;
    rdfs:comment "Short attention span is closely related to distractibility, as both involve difficulties in maintaining focus."@en .

dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_0006979 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Sleep-wake cycle disturbance"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0006979 ;
    rdfs:comment "The phrase describes a decreased need for sleep, which aligns with sleep-wake cycle disturbances."@en .

dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_0033844 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Racing thoughts"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0033844 ;
    rdfs:comment "The phrase describes racing thoughts, which directly corresponds to the HPO term for Racing thoughts."@en .

dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_4000092 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Poor conversational reciprocity"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.8 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_4000092 ;
    rdfs:comment "The phrase describes increased talkativeness, which relates to poor conversational reciprocity."@en .

dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_5200270 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Grandiosity"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_5200270 ;
    rdfs:comment "Grandiosity directly corresponds to the concept of inflated self-esteem or grandiosity in the DSM-5 criteria."@en .

dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_C a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Bipolar II Disorder — Criterion C"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The episode is associated with an unequivocal change in functioning that is uncharacteristic of the individual when not symptomatic"@en .

dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_D a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Bipolar II Disorder — Criterion D"@en ;
    med:hasSymptom dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_D__HP_0000751 ;
    rdfs:comment "The disturbance in mood and the change in functioning are observable by others"@en .

dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_D__HP_0000751 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Personality changes"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.8 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0000751 ;
    rdfs:comment "Personality changes can be associated with observable disturbances in mood and functioning."@en .

dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_E a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Bipolar II Disorder — Criterion E"@en ;
    med:hasSymptom dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_E__HP_0031844 ;
    rdfs:comment "The episode is not severe enough to cause marked impairment in social or occupational functioning or to necessitate hospitalization. If there are psychotic features, the episode is, by definition, manic"@en .

dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_E__HP_0031844 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Euphoria"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.8 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0031844 ;
    rdfs:comment "Euphoria is a key feature of manic episodes, aligning with the description of the episode in the DSM-5 criteria."@en .

dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_F a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Bipolar II Disorder — Criterion F"@en ;
    med:hasSymptom dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_F__HP_0100754 ;
    rdfs:comment "The episode is not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance (e.g., a drug of abuse, a medication, other treatment) or another medical condition **Note:** A full hypomanic episode that emerges during antidepressant treatment (e.g., medication, electroconvulsive therapy) but persists at a fully syndromal level beyond the physiological effect of that treatment is sufficient evidence for a hypomanic episode diagnosis. However, caution is indicated so that one or two symptoms (particularly increased irritability, edginess, or agitation following antidepressant use) are not taken as sufficient for diagnosis of a hypomanic episode, nor necessarily indicative of a bipolar diathesis."@en .

dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_F__HP_0100754 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Mania"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0100754 ;
    rdfs:comment "The phrase discusses a hypomanic episode, which is closely related to mania, making this the best matching term."@en .

dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_G a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Bipolar II Disorder — Criterion G"@en ;
    med:hasMinimumSymptomCount 5 ;
    med:hasRequiredDuration "P14D"^^xsd:duration ;
    med:hasSymptom dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_G__HP_0000713,
        dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_G__HP_0012154,
        dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_G__HP_0012378,
        dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_G__HP_0031987,
        dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_G__HP_0100785,
        dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_G__HP_5200273 ;
    rdfs:comment "Five (or more) of the following symptoms have been present during the same 2-week period and represent a change from previous functioning; at least one of the symptoms is either (1) depressed mood or (2) loss of interest or pleasure **Note:** Do not include symptoms that are clearly attributable to a medical condition. Depressed mood most of the day, nearly every day, as indicated by either subjective report (e.g., feels sad, empty, or hopeless) or observation made by others (e.g., appears tearful). (**Note:** In children and adolescents, can be irritable mood.) Markedly diminished interest or pleasure in all, or almost all, activities most of the day, nearly every day (as indicated by either subjective account or observation). Significant weight loss when not dieting or weight gain (e.g., a change of more than 5% of body weight in a month), or decrease or increase in appetite nearly every day. (**Note:** In children, consider failure to make expected weight gain.) Insomnia or hypersomnia nearly every day. Psychomotor agitation or retardation nearly every day (observable by others, not merely subjective feelings of restlessness or being slowed down). Fatigue or loss of energy nearly every day. Feelings of worthlessness or excessive or inappropriate guilt (which may be delusional) nearly every day (not merely self-reproach or guilt about being sick). Diminished ability to think or concentrate, or indecisiveness, nearly every day (either by subjective account or as observed by others). Recurrent thoughts of death (not just fear of dying), recurrent suicidal ideation without a specific plan, or a suicide attempt or a specific plan for committing suicide."@en .

dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_G__HP_0000713 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Agitation"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0000713 ;
    rdfs:comment "Agitation is a direct match for psychomotor agitation, which is a key component of the DSM criterion."@en .

dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_G__HP_0012154 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Anhedonia"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 1.0 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0012154 ;
    rdfs:comment "Anhedonia directly corresponds to the diminished interest or pleasure in activities described in the DSM-5 criterion."@en .

dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_G__HP_0012378 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Fatigue"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 1.0 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0012378 ;
    rdfs:comment "The term 'Fatigue' directly corresponds to the clinical phrase describing fatigue or loss of energy."@en .

dsm5:Bipolar_II_Disorder_Criterion_G__HP_0031987 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Diminished ability to concentrate"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
