dsm5:Inhalant_Intoxication_Criterion_D a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Inhalant Intoxication — Criterion D"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The signs or symptoms are not attributable to another medical condition and are not better explained by another mental disorder, including intoxication with another substance. Coding note: The ICD-10-CM code depends on whether there is a comorbid inhalant use disorder. If a mild inhalant use disorder is comorbid, the ICD-10-CM code is F18.120, and if a moderate or severe inhalant use disorder is comorbid, the ICD-10-CM code is F18.220. If there is no comorbid inhalant use disorder, then the ICD-10-CM code is F18.920"@en .

dsm5:Inhalant_Use_Disorder_Criterion_A a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Inhalant Use Disorder — Criterion A"@en ;
    med:hasMinimumSymptomCount 2 ;
    med:hasRequiredDuration "P1Y"^^xsd:duration ;
    med:hasSymptom dsm5:Inhalant_Use_Disorder_Criterion_A__HP_0007086,
        dsm5:Inhalant_Use_Disorder_Criterion_A__HP_0033511,
        dsm5:Inhalant_Use_Disorder_Criterion_A__HP_6000568 ;
    rdfs:comment "A problematic pattern of use of a hydrocarbon-based inhalant substance leading to clinically significant impairment or distress, as manifested by at least two of the following, occurring within a 12-month period The inhalant substance is often taken in larger amounts or over a longer period than was intended. There is a persistent desire or unsuccessful efforts to cut down or control use of the inhalant substance. A great deal of time is spent in activities necessary to obtain the inhalant substance, use it, or recover from its effects. Craving, or a strong desire or urge to use the inhalant substance. Recurrent use of the inhalant substance resulting in a failure to fulfill major role obligations at work, school, or home. Continued use of the inhalant substance despite having persistent or recurrent social or interpersonal problems caused or exacerbated by the effects of its use. Important social, occupational, or recreational activities are given up or reduced because of use of the inhalant substance. Recurrent use of the inhalant substance in situations in which it is physically hazardous. Use of the inhalant substance is continued despite knowledge of having a persistent or recurrent physical or psychological problem that is likely to have been caused or exacerbated by the substance. Tolerance, as defined by either of the following: A need for markedly increased amounts of the inhalant substance to achieve intoxication or desired effect. A markedly diminished effect with continued use of the same amount of the inhalant substance."@en .

dsm5:Inhalant_Use_Disorder_Criterion_A__HP_0007086 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Social and occupational deterioration"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0007086 ;
    rdfs:comment "The phrase describes a reduction in social and occupational activities due to inhalant substance use, which aligns with social and occupational deterioration."@en .

dsm5:Inhalant_Use_Disorder_Criterion_A__HP_0033511 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Addictive substance use"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0033511 ;
    rdfs:comment "The phrase describes a persistent desire or unsuccessful efforts to control substance use, which aligns with the concept of addictive substance use."@en .

dsm5:Inhalant_Use_Disorder_Criterion_A__HP_6000568 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Elevated blood ethylene glycol concentration"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.8 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_6000568 ;
    rdfs:comment "Increased amounts of the inhalant substance may lead to elevated blood concentrations of substances like ethylene glycol, indicating a need for increased intake to achieve desired effects."@en .

