dsm5:Pica_Criterion_A__HP_0100738 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Abnormal eating behavior"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0100738 ;
    rdfs:comment "The phrase describes a behavior related to eating, specifically abnormal eating behavior, which aligns with the selected HPO term."@en .

dsm5:Pica_Criterion_B a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Pica — Criterion B"@en ;
    med:hasSymptom dsm5:Pica_Criterion_B__HP_0100738 ;
    rdfs:comment "The eating of nonnutritive, nonfood substances is inappropriate to the developmental level of the individual"@en .

dsm5:Pica_Criterion_B__HP_0100738 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Abnormal eating behavior"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0100738 ;
    rdfs:comment "The phrase describes inappropriate eating behavior, which aligns with the term 'Abnormal eating behavior'."@en .

dsm5:Pica_Criterion_C a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Pica — Criterion C"@en ;
    med:hasSymptom dsm5:Pica_Criterion_C__HP_0100738 ;
    rdfs:comment "The eating behavior is not part of a culturally supported or socially normative practice"@en .

dsm5:Pica_Criterion_C__HP_0100738 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Abnormal eating behavior"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0100738 ;
    rdfs:comment "The phrase describes eating behavior that deviates from cultural norms, which aligns with abnormal eating behavior."@en .

dsm5:Pica_Criterion_D a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Pica — Criterion D"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "If the eating behavior occurs in the context of another mental disorder (e.g., intellectual developmental disorder \\[intellectual disability\\], autism spectrum disorder, schizophrenia) or medical condition (including pregnancy), it is sufficiently severe to warrant additional clinical attention. Coding note: The ICD-10-CM codes for pica are F98.3 in children and F50.89 in adults"@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_A a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Posttraumatic Stress Disorder — Criterion A"@en ;
    med:hasMinimumSymptomCount 1 ;
    med:hasSymptom dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_A__HP_0032316,
        dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_A__HP_0032942 ;
    rdfs:comment "Exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence in one (or more) of the following ways Directly experiencing the traumatic event(s). Witnessing, in person, the event(s) as it occurred to others. Learning that the traumatic event(s) occurred to a close family member or close friend. In cases of actual or threatened death of a family member or friend, the event(s) must have been violent or accidental. Experiencing repeated or extreme exposure to aversive details of the traumatic event(s) (e.g., first responders collecting human remains; police officers repeatedly exposed to details of child abuse). **Note:** Criterion A4 does not apply to exposure through electronic media, television, movies, or pictures, unless this exposure is work related."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_A__HP_0032316 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Family history"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.8 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0032316 ;
    rdfs:comment "The phrase refers to learning about traumatic events affecting close family members or friends, which relates to family history."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_A__HP_0032942 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Avoidance of stimuli associated with traumatic event"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.8 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0032942 ;
    rdfs:comment "The phrase relates to witnessing traumatic events, which aligns with avoidance of stimuli associated with traumatic events."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_B a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Posttraumatic Stress Disorder — Criterion B"@en ;
    med:hasMinimumSymptomCount 1 ;
    med:hasRequiredDuration "P6Y"^^xsd:duration ;
    med:hasSymptom dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_0032938,
        dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_0032940,
        dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_0032941,
        dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_5200104 ;
    rdfs:comment "Presence of one (or more) of the following intrusion symptoms associated with the traumatic event(s), beginning after the traumatic event(s) occurred Recurrent, involuntary, and intrusive distressing memories of the traumatic event(s). **Note:** In children older than 6 years, repetitive play may occur in which themes or aspects of the traumatic event(s) are expressed. Recurrent distressing dreams in which the content and/or affect of the dream are related to the traumatic event(s). **Note:** In children, there may be frightening dreams without recognizable content. Dissociative reactions (e.g., flashbacks) in which the individual feels or acts as if the traumatic event(s) were recurring. (Such reactions may occur on a continuum, with the most extreme expression being a complete loss of awareness of present surroundings.) **Note:** In children, trauma-specific reenactment may occur in play. Intense or prolonged psychological distress at exposure to internal or external cues that symbolize or resemble an aspect of the traumatic event(s). Marked physiological reactions to internal or external cues that symbolize or resemble an aspect of the traumatic event(s)."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_0032938 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Recurrent trauma-related distressing dreams"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0032938 ;
    rdfs:comment "The phrase describes recurrent distressing dreams related to traumatic events, which aligns closely with the concept of trauma-related distressing dreams."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_0032940 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Dissociation"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0032940 ;
    rdfs:comment "Dissociative reactions, such as flashbacks, are directly related to the concept of dissociation, making this the best match."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_0032941 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Intense psychological distress to cues"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0032941 ;
    rdfs:comment "The term 'Intense psychological distress to cues' closely aligns with the description of psychological distress related to cues that symbolize or resemble traumatic events."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_B__HP_5200104 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Abnormal play"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.8 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_5200104 ;
    rdfs:comment "Abnormal play is relevant as it encompasses repetitive play themes related to traumatic events."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_C a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Posttraumatic Stress Disorder — Criterion C"@en ;
    med:hasSymptom dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_C__HP_0002354 ;
    rdfs:comment "Persistent avoidance of stimuli associated with the traumatic event(s), beginning after the traumatic event(s) occurred, as evidenced by one or both of the following Avoidance of or efforts to avoid distressing memories, thoughts, or feelings about or closely associated with the traumatic event(s). Avoidance of or efforts to avoid external reminders (people, places, conversations, activities, objects, situations) that arouse distressing memories, thoughts, or feelings about or closely associated with the traumatic event(s)."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_C__HP_0002354 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Memory impairment"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.8 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0002354 ;
    rdfs:comment "Memory impairment is relevant as the avoidance behavior is often linked to distressing memories associated with trauma."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_D a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Posttraumatic Stress Disorder — Criterion D"@en ;
    med:hasMinimumSymptomCount 2 ;
    med:hasSymptom dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_D__HP_0030213,
        dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_D__HP_0031433,
        dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_D__HP_0031467,
        dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_D__HP_5200213,
        dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_D__HP_5200217,
        dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_D__HP_5200271 ;
    rdfs:comment "Negative alterations in cognitions and mood associated with the traumatic event(s), beginning or worsening after the traumatic event(s) occurred, as evidenced by two (or more) of the following Inability to remember an important aspect of the traumatic event(s) (typically due to dissociative amnesia and not to other factors such as head injury, alcohol, or drugs). Persistent and exaggerated negative beliefs or expectations about oneself, others, or the world (e.g., \"I am bad,\" \"No one can be trusted,\" \"The world is completely dangerous,\" \"My whole nervous system is permanently ruined\"). Persistent, distorted cognitions about the cause or consequences of the traumatic event(s) that lead the individual to blame himself/herself or others. Persistent negative emotional state (e.g., fear, horror, anger, guilt, or shame). Markedly diminished interest or participation in significant activities. Feelings of detachment or estrangement from others. Persistent inability to experience positive emotions (e.g., inability to experience happiness, satisfaction, or loving feelings)."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_D__HP_0030213 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Emotional dearth"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0030213 ;
    rdfs:comment "Emotional dearth closely aligns with the inability to experience positive emotions as described in the DSM criterion."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_D__HP_0031433 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Alexithymia"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.8 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0031433 ;
    rdfs:comment "Alexithymia relates to difficulties in identifying and expressing emotions, which can connect to distorted cognitions about trauma and self-blame."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_D__HP_0031467 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Dysregulated negative emotional state"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0031467 ;
    rdfs:comment "Dysregulated negative emotional state closely aligns with the persistent negative emotional states described in the DSM criterion."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_D__HP_5200213 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Diminishment of emotional responsiveness"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_5200213 ;
    rdfs:comment "The term 'Diminishment of emotional responsiveness' closely relates to the concept of diminished interest or participation in activities, reflecting a lack of engagement."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_D__HP_5200217 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Depersonalization"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_5200217 ;
    rdfs:comment "Depersonalization closely relates to feelings of detachment or estrangement from others."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_D__HP_5200271 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Hopelessness"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_5200271 ;
    rdfs:comment "The phrase describes persistent negative beliefs and expectations, which aligns closely with the concept of hopelessness."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_E a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Posttraumatic Stress Disorder — Criterion E"@en ;
    med:hasMinimumSymptomCount 2 ;
    med:hasSymptom dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_E__HP_0002267,
        dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_E__HP_0002360,
        dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_E__HP_0031987,
        dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_E__HP_5200231 ;
    rdfs:comment "Marked alterations in arousal and reactivity associated with the traumatic event(s), beginning or worsening after the traumatic event(s) occurred, as evidenced by two (or more) of the following Irritable behavior and angry outbursts (with little or no provocation) typically expressed as verbal or physical aggression toward people or objects. Reckless or self-destructive behavior. Hypervigilance. Exaggerated startle response. Problems with concentration. Sleep disturbance (e.g., difficulty falling or staying asleep or restless sleep)."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_E__HP_0002267 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Exaggerated startle response"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 1.0 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0002267 ;
    rdfs:comment "The term 'Exaggerated startle response' directly matches the clinical phrase."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_E__HP_0002360 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Sleep disturbance"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0002360 ;
    rdfs:comment "The phrase describes a general sleep disturbance, which directly matches the HPO term for sleep disturbance."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_E__HP_0031987 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Diminished ability to concentrate"@en ;
    dsm5:confidence 0.9 ;
    med:mapsToHPO hpo:HP_0031987 ;
    rdfs:comment "Diminished ability to concentrate directly relates to problems with concentration."@en .

dsm5:Posttraumatic_Stress_Disorder_Criterion_E__HP_5200231 a dsm5:Symptom,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Hypervigilance"@en ;
