dsm5:Prolonged_Grief_Disorder_Criterion_C a dsm5:DiagnosticCriterion,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Prolonged Grief Disorder — Criterion C"@en ;
    med:hasMinimumSymptomCount 3 ;
    med:hasSymptom dsm5:Prolonged_Grief_Disorder_Criterion_C__HP_0030213,
        dsm5:Prolonged_Grief_Disorder_Criterion_C__HP_0032940,
        dsm5:Prolonged_Grief_Disorder_Criterion_C__HP_0032942,
        dsm5:Prolonged_Grief_Disorder_Criterion_C__HP_5200273,
        dsm5:Prolonged_Grief_Disorder_Criterion_C__HP_5200320 ;
    rdfs:comment "Since the death, at least three of the following symptoms have been present most days to a clinically significant degree. In addition, the symptoms have occurred nearly every day for at least the last month Identity disruption (e.g., feeling as though part of oneself has died) since the death. Marked sense of disbelief about the death. Avoidance of reminders that the person is dead (in children and adolescents, may be characterized by efforts to avoid reminders). Intense emotional pain (e.g., anger, bitterness, sorrow) related to the death. Difficulty reintegrating into one's relationships and activities after the death (e.g., problems engaging with friends, pursuing interests, or planning for the future). Emotional numbness (absence or marked reduction of emotional experience) as a result of the death. Feeling that life is meaningless as a result of the death. Intense loneliness as a result of the death."@en .

